=== MISSION (immutable plan — your standing directive) ===
MISSION MODE: build
Harden the prod-coordination gate cause-first, then route every /mission lane to a model matched to
the difficulty of its work. ALL work is in ~/.claude-dotfiles. NOTHING touches the dental app.

Settled decisions live in tmp/briefs/2026-08-16-mission-model-routing-and-prod-gate.md (read FIRST).
Detector-half detail in tmp/ready-plans/2026-08-16-prod-classifier-residual-fix.md.

PART 1 - lock lifecycle (the root cause). The prod lock is never released by anything, not even
after a genuine production action; its only exit is aging past a 15-minute TTL into "stale", which
requires a human to delete the file. Two windows hit this twice in three days. Add the holder pid,
release on command completion, allow reclaim only when the holder process is provably dead. This
converts every future misclassification from an hours-long machine-wide block into seconds.

PART 2 - detector accuracy. The interpreter bail-out matches -niE because the character class is
case-insensitive, so a SEARCH for dangerous strings disabled stripping entirely. And the interpreter
check runs BEFORE heredoc stripping, so a document's own content vetoes stripping of its container.
Fix both; every new fixture watched failing against the pre-fix files first.

PART 3 - ledger cleanup. Delete the 11 owner-approved contaminated rows from the production log.
Back up first; other windows append concurrently, so snapshot, filter, re-check the tail, then swap.

PART 4 - model routing. Hierarchy: Opus 4.8 xhigh conductor = authority and continuity (owner-set at
launch; the playbook ASSERTS it, it cannot select it). Opus = difficult judgment and difficult
implementation. Sonnet = strong production workforce. Codex = cheap independent research, review,
verification, and mechanical implementation. Includes three INDEPENDENT plan reviewers with
deliberately different prompts (Opus broad / Codex xhigh executability / Codex high value-critic),
an Opus-medium meta-review, three implementation classes chosen by the conductor before dispatch,
the Codex panel pinned to High rather than inheriting from global config, and Claude lenses on
Sonnet / Sonnet / Opus-medium.

PART 5 - make the files match. Update all 14 agent definitions so they stop uniformly claiming
model: opus / effort: high, plus commands/mission.md, plan.md, implement.md, codex-review.md,
scripts/codex-exec.sh and codex-build-chunk.sh. Then run the dotfiles suites and verify: mission
resumes across compaction; every expected reviewer actually launches; Codex High is really High and
not inherited; the two Codex plan lanes use materially different prompts; Codex write access stays
available ONLY through the guarded linked-worktree wrapper; Sonnet workers cannot silently become
mission authority; convergence still requires every required lane; a missing or dead reviewer still
VOIDs the round instead of creating false convergence.

Known drift to fix regardless: two docs claim the Codex config effort is "max" when it is "high".

Standing directive: route substantial work through research -> /plan(+reviewers) -> /implement ->
/codex-review, looping to 2 honest dry rounds (independent reviewers judge dryness); soft targets
plan 4-6 / codex 3-6, cap per 6.1; /pre-compact freely interleaved; active until a
[mission] MISSION-CLEARED line appears in the LOG.
